## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of approximate quantiles with streaming data, a common constraint in production systems. It compares simple histograms to exact tracking and asks for concrete complexity and error bounds.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms for quantiles
- Per-element O(1) updates, O(W) queries
- Error bounds from bucket width w

## Code Example
```javascript
function HistQuantile(m, W) {
  const bucket = new Array(W).fill(0);
  this.n = 0; this.m = m; this.W = W;
  const w = Math.ceil(m / W);
  this.update = (x) => { const idx = Math.min(Math.floor(x / w), W-1); bucket[idx]++; this.n++; };
  this.median = () => {
    let target = Math.floor(this.n / 2) + 1;
    let cum = 0; let i = 0; const bw = Math.ceil(m / W);
    for (; i < W; i++) { if (cum + bucket[i] >= target) break; cum += bucket[i]; }
    const L = i * bw; const left = target - cum;
    const est = left <= bucket[i] ? L + (left / bucket[i]) * bw : L + bw;
    return est;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust for skewed data?  
- How does choosing W affect error vs memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming distinct counting under memory constraints, a common real-world need in chat pipelines. Introduces HyperLogLog beyond basic frequency sketches and checks ability to reason about merging across shards.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Memory-constant sketch with m registers and 5–6 bits per register
- Per-element update and merge operations (max per register)
- Relative error ~1.04/√m; small-bias corrections for tiny cardinalities
- Trade-offs: m vs accuracy vs memory; cross-shard merging

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateHLL(M, xHash64) {
  const p = M.length ? Math.log2(M.length) : 0;
  const i = xHash64 >>> (64 - p);
  const w = (xHash64 << p) >>> 0;
  const v = 1 + Math.clz32(~w); // rough trailing zeros proxy
  M[i] = Math.max(M[i], v);
}

function estimateCardinality(M) {
  const m = M.length;
  const sum = M.reduce((acc, x) => acc + Math.pow(2, -x), 0);
  const alpha = (m === 16) ? 0.673 : (m === 32) ? 0.697 : (m === 64) ? 0.709 : 0.721;
  return (alpha * m * m) / sum;
}
```
